  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday's results land within Halloween's expected 10% drag, but three operational patterns demand attention.\n\nPort Washington stayed flat despite losing bather coverage entirely\u2014core groom revenue fell 16%, but retail surged 61% on higher transaction count, masking soft grooming performance. This is a capacity story: you ran 4 groomers at baseline productivity, but zero bathers means grooms are handling their own preps, which throttles throughput. The add-on attach rate collapsed from 71% to 59%, signaling the team is focused on throughput over upsell when bathers are absent. Hicksville moved the right direction\u20143 groomers hit $488/groomer, 17% above their 30-day baseline\u2014but retail revenue halved, which suggests front-of-house retail discipline slipped or traffic composition shifted toward service-only customers. Glen Cove is the concern: 1 groomer produced $185, well below the $231 baseline, and you've scheduled zero groomer-days for the next seven days. That's a hard stop on revenue at that location.\n\nThe bright spot: Port Washington's year-to-date is +36% revenue and +35% appointments versus last year\u2014you're building real momentum on the core business. The lapse pools at Port ($350K annual value, 559 customers) and Hicksville ($160K, 220 customers) represent material upside if you activate even 20% this quarter.\n\nToday Port has 1 groomer and 15 bather hours; tomorrow jumps to 5 groomers and 15 bathers. Next 7 days across both active locations: 58 groomer-days and 83 bather hours."
